So after a long time playing with Dscript, the 2D writing system, it has=20
finally evolved a form for 3D writing.

Now 3D may seem intimidating, I know it did to me, with all the complexity=
=20
of Dscript 2D writing, I was always scared to try 3D as I expected the=20
complexity would be impossible to deal with,

Over the last few months I have toyed with some ideas, and finally I came=
=20
up with a solution. The solution was actually quite simple. Just "ignore"=
=20
all the dimensions and use them for "flexibility" as opposed to "value".

WireScript is a "1D" writing system, I say that because it is just a string=
=20
of trinary values that can be represented by bends and curves. There are=20
only 3 possible values, and they are combined into groups of 3 to allow an=
=20
index of 27 (26 letters+space).

WireScript requires absolutely no "pen lifts" this means it can be written=
=20
with wires and exist in a full 3D environment.

With WireScript your can turn text into 3D sculptures, ornaments, and=20
designs. Or remain in a 2D environment if you want and make flat designs=20
that can made with wires or a pen and paper.

So much fun, so many permutations. It has even given me a new appreciation=
=20
for the complexities of protein folding.

--=20
